@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
' SPEC source: cmd/proveo/main.go (execWithEgress · cleanup · onSignalCleanup ·
' captureSidecarLogs · the Squid log-dir chmod) and internal/egress/exec.go.
title Bring-up and teardown — who owns cleanup when a signal skips every defer

node "cmd/proveo — execWithEgress" as EXEC <<host>> {
  component "sync.Once cleanup\nregistered BEFORE staging" as ONCE <<app>>
  component "onSignalCleanup\nSIGINT · SIGTERM · SIGHUP → exit 130" as SIG <<app>>
  component "captureSidecarLogs\nbefore anything is removed" as LOGS <<app>>
  component "stage: squid config · log dir\nmitmproxy confdir · flows" as STAGE <<app>>
}

node "internal/egress — Plan" as PLAN <<host>> {
  component "Apply(Runner)\nnetworks · sidecars · connects" as APPLY <<app>>
  component "WaitSquidReady\n/dev/tcp probe on :3128" as WSQ <<app>>
  component "WaitOllamaReady\ndocker exec ollama list" as WOL <<app>>
  component "waitForFile\ninspector CA" as WCA <<app>>
  component "Teardown(Runner)\nquiet runner — expected errors stay quiet" as DOWN <<app>>
}

component "assemble(assembleInput)\n→ Plan + runner.Config" as ASM <<app>>
component "dind.Sidecar.Cleanup\nNOT safe to call concurrently" as DIND <<app>>
database "<egDir>/inject/broker.env\n0600, must not outlive the run" as INJECT <<db>>
component "Agent container\ndocker run -it" as AGENT <<app>>
component "Leaked sidecar · network · secret\nsignal skipped every defer" as LEAK <<error>>
component "Cold-start race\nagent's first request beats Squid" as RACE <<error>>

ASM ARROW_MAIN STAGE : everything below is applied, nothing is decided
STAGE ARROW_MAIN APPLY
ONCE ARROW_MAIN SIG : same once-guarded func, either path
APPLY ARROW_MAIN WSQ
APPLY ARROW_OPTIONAL WOL : local-model add-on
APPLY ARROW_OPTIONAL WCA : firewall tier only
WSQ ARROW_MAIN AGENT : only once ready
WSQ ARROW_ERROR RACE : without the probe
AGENT ARROW_QUEUE LOGS : on exit
LOGS ARROW_MAIN DOWN : order is load-bearing — ""--rm"" discards them
DOWN ARROW_MAIN DIND
DOWN ARROW_MAIN INJECT : remove
SIG ARROW_ERROR LEAK : what this guards against

note left of ASM
  **The plan is decided before this path starts, and deciding it touches
  nothing.** ""assemble"" reads only its resolved inputs — no environment,
  no filesystem, no exec — so the topology and the agent's run config can
  be asserted in a unit test with no Docker present. Everything below
  this line is execution: it may fail on the host, and that is why it
  needs the cleanup contract rather than a return value.
end note

note right of ONCE
  **Teardown is owned by the exec path, not by the caller that built the
  plan.** Two branches own it — ""execWithEgress"" for a run with an egress
  topology, and a signal-safe branch for the bare path — and exactly one
  of them always runs after a successful start, with no early return
  between. Registering the cleanup //before// any staging is what makes an
  early failure still tear down what ""doRun"" already brought up, which in
  practice means the privileged DinD sidecar.

  It must be once-guarded and nil-safe: it can fire from the signal
  goroutine while the normal-return defer runs it too, and most runs have
  no DinD sidecar to clean up at all.

  **The awkward case is DinD without a topology** — brokered credentials
  and no local model. There is no lifecycle teardown to hang it on, but a
  privileged sidecar must still come down on a signal, so the same single
  guarded function backs both the defer and the handler. ""Cleanup"" is not
  safe to call concurrently, so //one// guarded entry point is not a
  stylistic choice.
end note

note bottom of SIG
  **Go does not run deferred functions when a signal ends the process.**
  Every ""defer"" in the run is therefore useless for the case an operator
  is most likely to cause: Ctrl-C. Anything that outlives the process if
  skipped — the egress topology, the injected secret, a privileged
  sidecar — needs this handler instead.

  It runs the cleanup and exits 130 (the shell's convention for
  SIGINT), and returns a stop func so the caller can deregister it on the
  normal path.
end note

note right of LOGS
  **The sidecars' output is captured before teardown because there is no
  second copy.** They run detached with ""--rm"", so ""docker logs"" is the
  only record and it disappears with the container.

  That record is what names //which// component refused a request and
  why. Without it a 403 from Squid is indistinguishable from the
  provider's own 401 — the exact confusion this capture exists to end. It
  runs on the teardown path, including after a failure, so it is
  best-effort throughout: it must never itself be the reason a run
  reports an error.
end note

note right of WSQ
  **Squid is the internet-facing upstream that both enforced tiers transit**,
  so it is the one sidecar the agent cannot start ahead of: the probe waits
  for it to accept connections on :3128 before ""docker run"", which is what
  keeps the agent's first request from racing a cold Squid.
end note

note bottom of STAGE
  **Only what the plan needs is staged** — Squid config and log dir when
  a Squid sidecar exists, the mitmproxy confdir and flow dir only on the
  firewall tier.

  **The Squid log dir is world-writable, and only Linux reveals why.**
  Squid starts as root and drops to its own ""proxy"" user (uid 13) to
  write ""access\.log"" and ""cache\.log"". On Linux a bind mount preserves
  host ownership, so a directory owned by the invoking host uid at 0755
  is //not// writable by uid 13: Squid exits during startup, ""--rm"" leaves
  it "marked for removal", and the network-connect inside ""Apply"" fails
  with nothing pointing at permissions. Docker Desktop on macOS makes
  bind mounts permissive, which is why this reproduces on Linux hosts
  only.

  World-write is acceptable for a per-user, per-session state directory.
  The egress-proxy directories stay 0755 because that sidecar runs as
  the host uid and already owns them.
end note

note bottom of DOWN
  **Teardown and readiness probes run on a quiet runner.** Both
  legitimately hit transient docker errors: "No such container" once a
  ""--rm"" sidecar has already removed itself, "connection refused" while
  Squid is still binding :3128. Those lines are expected, and letting
  docker's stderr reach the operator's terminal makes a healthy run look
  broken.

  ""Apply"" keeps stderr on. Its failures are real and must be seen — the
  distinction is between an error that is part of the protocol and one
  that ends the run.
end note

note right of DIND
  Attached to the agent's user-defined network so the agent resolves
  ""docker"" by alias — the broker-plus-local-model case. On the default
  bridge the same reachability is wired with ""--link"" instead. A no-op
  when the run has no sidecar or no network.
end note
@enduml
